# Descriptor authoring

The descriptor is the contract. The file is
`src/{{ package_name }}/descriptor.json`. Every command must trace to evidence.
Do not invent commands or capabilities.

## Validate

- `benchweave-sdk check src/{{ package_name }}/descriptor.json` — the offline
  check for schema and basic semantics.

## Per-pin validation

The SDK does not validate against one OTDP schema. It validates each
descriptor against the version in the descriptor's own `otdp_version` field. A
descriptor pinned to an older version validates against that version's own
schemas. The SDK never reinterprets a pin through the active version.

## Structural rules

- `capabilities` must equal the keys of `operations` (S01).
- Parameter names must be unique (S01). Parameter bounds must not be reversed
  (S02).
- The offline check is partial. It covers S01, S02 and S04. Record the full
  S01–S18, C01–C12 and M01–M14 obligations and their evidence separately.

## Refusal codes

The check and validation surface refuse with machine-matchable codes:

- `version_not_served:` — this SDK does not carry the pinned version.
- `retired_identifier:` — the pin names a retired version.
- `provider_feature_missing:` — the provider `feature_id` is not in
  `required_features`.
- `provider_transport_undeclared:` — an `otdp.transport.*` feature is required
  but no provider on the custom transport declares it.
- `unknown_otdp_feature:` — an `otdp.*` feature id is neither corpus-known nor
  declared through a provider.
- `provider_contract_missing:` — the provider pin does not resolve to a
  contained regular file.
- `provider_contract_hash_mismatch:` — the pinned bytes do not hash to the
  pinned value.
- `provider_contract_invalid:` — the pinned contract document fails a contract
  check.

A yanked pin stays conforming and validates against its own bytes. A
deprecation warning names the move-to version.

## Read the docs

The plugin SDK guide covers this in section "3. Run the checks", section
